urrent contract, whether it should change, and why the proposed edit will not unintentionally alter unrelated paths. For each item, state whether the proposed behavior must change, must remain unchanged, or must remain backward-compatible at the public boundary; explicitly write the invariants for unchanged behavior, preserve error types and return shapes unless the issue requires otherwise, and add regression coverage for both the fixed case and the nearest unaffected cases., or is uncertain; turn every unchanged or uncertain case into a concrete validation check, and use any failure to refine the implementation before declaring the patch complete., or needs a compatibility test; use repository search to verify the map before editing. Use search and file inspection to verify each dependency rather than inferring it from names. For every behavior not explicitly targeted by the issue, identify an invariant that must remain unchanged and compare old versus new behavior on representative normal cases plus boundary and adversarial cases. Prefer preserving existing public semantics, exception types, shapes, units, ordering, and serialization formats unless the issue explicitly requires otherwise. Before editing, reproduce the reported behavior with the narrowest relevant test or command whenever feasible, capture the failure output, and cite the concrete code path from input to symptom. After each edit, rerun that reproducer first, then the nearest related tests; if the result differs from the hypothesis, inspect the new failure and iterate rather than declaring success. Trace the relevant data and control flow, state the likely root cause, and identify the smallest compatible change. Before implementation, enumerate the behavioral contract for valid, empty, null, missing, malformed, boundary, repeated, reordered, and unsupported inputs, including expected exceptions, defaults, mutation, ordering, precision, and backward compatibility. Make the patch satisfy the contract